<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="grv-ch-scissors">
  <title>Scissors relations and realizations</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Let <m>k</m> be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. A <em>variety</em> is a reduced separated scheme of finite type over <m>k</m>; it may be reducible, disconnected, singular, or nonproper. Cutting a variety into finitely many locally closed pieces suggests an additive invariant, while Cartesian product supplies multiplication. We construct the ring with these properties, determine what its additive completion means geometrically, and use fibrations to calculate classes. A Hodge polynomial then gives a way to prove that some of these classes are nonzero.</p>
  </introduction>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-definition">
    <title>Definition and universal property</title>
    <p>A closed subvariety and its open complement form the basic decomposition. Imposing additivity for these decompositions also gives additivity for every finite locally closed stratification, which will allow us to calculate with the pieces on which a geometric construction is simplest.</p>
    <definition xml:id="grv-def-ring">
      <title>Grothendieck ring of varieties</title>
      <idx>Grothendieck ring of varieties</idx><idx>scissors relation</idx><idx>Lefschetz class</idx>
      <statement><p>The abelian group <m>\Rk</m> is generated by symbols <m>[X]</m> for isomorphism classes of varieties, with relations
        <md number="no">[X]=[Z]+[X\setminus Z]</md>
        for closed reduced subvarieties <m>Z\subseteq X</m>. Multiplication is defined by <m>[X][Y]=[X\times_kY]</m>. We write <m>1=[\Spec k]</m>, <m>0=[\varnothing]</m>, and <m>\LL=[\A^1_k]</m>.</p></statement>
    </definition>
    <p>The product of two reduced finite-type schemes over the perfect field <m>k</m> is reduced. Multiplying a scissors relation by <m>[Y]</m> gives the relation for <m>Z\times Y\subseteq X\times Y</m>, so multiplication descends to the quotient. The class of a point is its identity. Taking <m>Z=X</m> gives <m>[\varnothing]=0</m>, and taking a union of connected components gives additivity under disjoint unions. These verifications also describe exactly which invariants factor through the ring.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-prop-universal">Let <m>A</m> be a commutative ring with identity. An isomorphism-invariant function <m>e</m> on varieties defines a unital ring homomorphism <m>\Rk\to A</m> precisely when <m>e(\Spec k)=1</m> and
      <md number="no">e(X)=e(Z)+e(X\setminus Z),\qquad e(X\times Y)=e(X)e(Y)</md>
      for every closed <m>Z\subseteq X</m> and every pair <m>X,Y</m>. Extend <m>e</m> linearly to the free abelian group on variety classes. The first equality kills the defining scissors relations, so the extension descends to <m>\Rk</m>; the second makes it multiplicative on finite sums. Conversely, a unital ring homomorphism has these properties by the defining equalities in <m>\Rk</m>.</p>
    <p>Such a homomorphism is called a <em>motivic measure</em>. Its target may be an ordinary ring of numerical invariants; the term does not require a category of motives. The realizations with values in Grothendieck rings of motives will be constructed in <xref ref="grv-ch-motives"/>. Before choosing a realization, we can already extend closed-open additivity to the decompositions used in calculations.</p>
    <lemma xml:id="grv-lem-stratification">
      <statement><p>If <m>X=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^rX_i</m> is a finite partition into locally closed subvarieties, then <m>[X]=\sum_i[X_i]</m>. Consequently, defining the class of a constructible subset as the sum of the classes of its strata gives a class independent of the finite locally closed stratification.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>The defining relation permits cuts by closed subsets. We refine the given partition by a sequence of these cuts. Write each locally closed piece as <m>X_i=F_i\setminus G_i</m>, where <m>G_i\subseteq F_i</m> are closed in <m>X</m>. For example, take <m>F_i=\overline{X_i}</m> and <m>G_i=F_i\setminus X_i</m>. Since <m>X_i</m> is open in its closure, <m>G_i</m> is closed in <m>F_i</m>, hence in <m>X</m>.</p>
        <p>Starting with <m>X</m>, cut by the closed sets <m>F_1,G_1,\ldots,F_r,G_r</m> one at a time. A cut by <m>H</m> replaces each current piece <m>W</m> by <m>W\cap H</m> and <m>W\setminus H</m>. Both remain locally closed in <m>X</m>, and <m>W\cap H</m> is closed in <m>W</m>, so this cut gives one defining scissors relation. After all the cuts, let <m>W_\alpha</m> be the nonempty pieces. Repeated substitution gives
          <md number="no">[X]=\sum_\alpha[W_\alpha].</md>
          Membership in each <m>F_i</m> and <m>G_i</m> is constant on a piece <m>W_\alpha</m>. Consequently every such piece lies in exactly one <m>X_i</m>.</p>
        <p>Make the same cuts inside <m>X_i</m>. The resulting nonempty pieces are precisely the <m>W_\alpha</m> contained in <m>X_i</m>, so
          <md number="no">[X_i]=\sum_{W_\alpha\subseteq X_i}[W_\alpha].</md>
          Summing these equalities over <m>i</m> gives the assertion. Finally, two locally closed stratifications of a constructible set have a common refinement by their intersections. Applying the proved formula to each stratum shows that both sums equal the sum over this common refinement.</p></proof>
    </lemma>
    <p xml:id="grv-ex-nodal-cuspidal">For example, let <m>C</m> be an irreducible nodal rational projective cubic. Its normalization is <m>\PP^1</m>, and it is an isomorphism away from the node and its two preimages. Cutting off these finite sets gives
      <md number="no">[C]=[\PP^1]-2+1=\LL.</md>
      For a cuspidal rational projective cubic <m>C'</m>, the cusp has one preimage, and the same calculation gives <m>[C']=1+\LL</m>. Thus a singularity can change the class, while a cuspidal curve and its normalization can still have equal classes. The equality is obtained by comparing their open pieces and the removed points.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-rem-object-conventions">The definition accommodates such singular curves without adding extra relations. It would give the same ring if nonreduced finite-type schemes were allowed: the closed immersion <m>X_{\mathrm{red}}\hookrightarrow X</m> has empty complement, so <m>[X]=[X_{\mathrm{red}}]</m>. It also suffices to use quasiprojective generators, because finite affine stratifications express every variety using them, and common refinements make this expression independent of the stratification. A presentation using only projective varieties requires different relations; the characteristic-zero blowup presentation in <xref ref="grv-ch-birational"/> provides them.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-anchor-finiteness">Finite type, however, prevents an additive collapse. If one enlarged the objects to allow a countable disjoint union <m>S</m> of points, then
      <md xml:id="margin-grv-finiteness" number="no">S\simeq S\sqcup\Spec k\quad\Longrightarrow\quad [S]=[S]+1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 1=0.</md>
      This is a consequence of that enlarged definition; no such infinite union is a generator of <m>\Rk</m>.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-semiring">
    <title>Piecewise isomorphism and additive completion</title>
    <p>The normalization calculations compare isomorphic open pieces and finite sets. This suggests a geometric equivalence before additive inverses are introduced. Comparing it with equality in <m>\Rk</m> will show where an auxiliary variety can enter a scissors identity.</p>
    <definition xml:id="grv-def-piecewise">
      <title>Piecewise isomorphism</title>
      <idx>piecewise isomorphism</idx><idx>cut-and-paste problem</idx>
      <statement><p>Varieties <m>X,Y</m> are <em>piecewise isomorphic</em>, written <m>X\simeq_{\mathrm{pw}}Y</m>, if there are finite locally closed partitions <m>X=\bigsqcup_iX_i</m> and <m>Y=\bigsqcup_iY_i</m> with <m>X_i\simeq Y_i</m> for every <m>i</m>.</p></statement>
    </definition>
    <p>Common refinements show that this is an equivalence relation and that disjoint union and product descend to its equivalence classes. The resulting semiring <m>S_k</m> has the same generators and scissors relations as <m>\Rk</m>, with addition still given by disjoint union: cutting into locally closed pieces preserves piecewise isomorphism, and a piecewise isomorphism is itself a finite sequence of such cuts and identifications. Passing from this semiring to the ring introduces additive inverses.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grv-prop-stabilized-piecewise">
      <title>Equality after additive completion</title>
      <statement><p>For varieties <m>X,Y</m>, the equality <m>[X]=[Y]</m> in <m>\Rk</m> holds if and only if there is a variety <m>T</m> such that
        <md number="no">X\sqcup T\simeq_{\mathrm{pw}}Y\sqcup T.</md>
        Thus <m>\Rk</m> is the additive group completion of <m>S_k</m>.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>For any commutative monoid <m>S</m>, its group completion is represented by pairs <m>(a,b)</m>, where <m>(a,b)</m> and <m>(a',b')</m> are identified when there exists <m>c\in S</m> with <m>a+b'+c=a'+b+c</m>. The images of <m>a</m> and <m>a'</m> agree precisely when <m>a+c=a'+c</m> for some <m>c</m>. Apply this description to <m>S_k</m>; every element is represented by a variety because finite sums are represented by disjoint unions.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>The cut-and-paste question asks whether the common summand <m>T</m> can always be cancelled in this statement. It is an additive cancellation problem in <m>S_k</m>; cancelling a factor from a product in <m>\Rk</m> is a separate question. Both fail: <xref ref="grv-cor-cut-paste"/> gives the additive counterexample, and the same chapter constructs multiplicative zero divisors. See <xref ref="grv-bib-LiuSebag" detail="Sections 2 and 4"/> for the formulation of the cut-and-paste question and its geometric consequences.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-basic-calculations">
    <title>Fibrations and elementary calculations</title>
    <p>A fibration can be computed by cutting its base into pieces over which it is a product. For bundles, this reduces the calculation to affine and projective fibres. It also gives a way to calculate the class of a space defined by successive choices of vectors.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grv-prop-zariski-fibration">
      <statement><p>Suppose <m>f:X\to B</m> is a Zariski locally trivial fibration with fibre <m>F</m>. Then <m>[X]=[B][F]</m>. The same formula holds under the weaker assumption that there is a finite locally closed partition
        <md number="no">B=\bigsqcup_i B_i</md>
        and, for every <m>i</m>, an isomorphism <m>f^{-1}(B_i)\simeq B_i\times F</m> over <m>B_i</m>, with the same variety <m>F</m> for all strata.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>Under the partition hypothesis, the inverse images of the strata partition <m>X</m>. Applying <xref ref="grv-lem-stratification"/> to both partitions gives
        <md number="no">[X]=\sum_i[f^{-1}(B_i)]=\sum_i[B_i][F]=[B][F].</md>
        Thus a product decomposition over each stratum suffices.</p>
        <p>If <m>f</m> is Zariski locally trivial, choose a finite trivializing open cover <m>B=U_1\cup\cdots\cup U_r</m>, using quasicompactness. Set <m>B_i=U_i\setminus\bigcup_{j&lt;i}U_j</m> and discard empty pieces. These pieces form a finite locally closed partition, and each lies in a trivializing open set. Hence they satisfy the partition hypothesis.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>The strata in the second hypothesis need not be open. For example, the natural map <m>\mathbb G_m\sqcup\Spec k\to\A^1</m>, sending the point to <m>0</m>, is an isomorphism over each of the strata <m>\mathbb G_m</m> and <m>\{0\}</m>. It therefore satisfies the partition hypothesis with <m>F=\Spec k</m>. It is not Zariski locally trivial: the inverse image of any open neighborhood of <m>0</m> is disconnected, whereas the neighborhood itself is irreducible. The formula still gives <m>[\mathbb G_m]+1=\LL</m>.</p>
    <p>The fibre classes needed for bundles follow from the defining product and scissors relations. Since <m>\A^r=(\A^1)^r</m>, its class is <m>\LL^r</m>. For <m>r\ge1</m>, removing a hyperplane from <m>\PP^r</m> leaves <m>\A^r</m>, so induction starting at a point gives
      <md number="no">[\PP^r]=[\PP^{r-1}]+\LL^r=1+\LL+\cdots+\LL^r.</md>
      This is the class formula for the usual affine-cell decomposition of projective space.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-cor-bundles">A vector bundle <m>V\to B</m> of constant rank <m>r\ge1</m> is Zariski locally trivial with fibre <m>\A^r</m>, and its projectivization has fibre <m>\PP^{r-1}</m>. Applying the proposition gives
      <md number="no">[V]=\LL^r[B],\qquad [\PP(V)]=(1+\LL+\cdots+\LL^{r-1})[B].</md>
      These formulas require no global trivialization of either bundle.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-ex-general-linear">For an application involving successive bundles, let <m>F_i</m> be the variety of linearly independent ordered <m>i</m>-tuples in <m>k^n</m>, with <m>F_0=\Spec k</m>. Over <m>F_i</m>, the vectors already chosen span a rank-<m>i</m> subbundle <m>U_i</m> of the trivial rank-<m>n</m> bundle. Choosing the next vector outside this subbundle gives <m>F_{i+1}</m>. Subtraction of the two bundle classes yields
      <md number="no">[F_{i+1}]=[F_i\times\A^n]-[U_i]=(\LL^n-\LL^i)[F_i].</md>
      Since <m>F_n=\GL_n</m>, iteration gives
      <md number="no">[\GL_n]=\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}(\LL^n-\LL^i),\qquad [\GL_2]=(\LL^2-1)(\LL^2-\LL).</md>
      Each factor comes from a complement of subbundles, so the product formula follows from actual decompositions rather than a count of geometric points.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-ex-etale-cover">Geometric fibres alone do not give the hypothesis of the proposition. Over <m>\C</m>, the map <m>\mathbb G_m\to\mathbb G_m</m>, <m>t\mapsto t^2</m>, is finite étale with two points in every geometric fibre, but its source and target both have class <m>\LL-1</m>. A product formula for a two-point fibre would instead give <m>2(\LL-1)</m>. The Hodge polynomial constructed below distinguishes these expressions. Thus étale local triviality cannot replace Zariski local triviality in <xref ref="grv-prop-zariski-fibration"/>.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-hodge-realization">
    <title>Numerical realizations and nonvanishing</title>
    <p>The scissors and bundle calculations prove equalities, but detecting a nonzero difference requires an invariant that respects the relations. Over <m>\C</m>, compactly supported cohomology gives the Hodge–Deligne polynomial. In characteristic zero its numerical form can also be defined from algebraic Hodge numbers of smooth projective varieties and the blowup presentation. We use the resulting realization as an external theorem here; its construction is explained in <xref ref="grv-ch-motives"/> and <xref ref="grf-hodge-polynomial"/>. See also <xref ref="grv-bib-CLNS" detail="Chapter 2, Section 3"/>.</p>
    <theorem xml:id="grv-thm-hodge-realization">
      <title>Virtual Hodge polynomial</title>
      <idx>Hodge–Deligne polynomial</idx><idx>motivic measure</idx>
      <statement><p>There is a ring homomorphism <m>E_k:\Rk\to\Z[u,v]</m> whose value on a smooth projective variety is
        <md number="no">E_k([X];u,v)=\sum_{p,q}(-1)^{p+q}\dim_kH^q(X,\Omega_X^p)u^pv^q.</md>
        It sends <m>\LL</m> to <m>uv</m>. For <m>k=\C</m> and arbitrary <m>X</m>, its definition is the alternating sum of the Hodge numbers of the mixed Hodge structures on <m>H_c^i(X,\Q)</m>.</p></statement>
    </theorem>
    <p>On smooth projective varieties, Künneth and the blowup formulas verify multiplicativity and the Bittner relation. Bittner's presentation, <xref ref="grv-thm-bittner"/>, then gives the characteristic-zero extension. In the complex definition the alternating sum over cohomological degrees is essential, since individual Betti numbers are not additive under a closed-open decomposition. The highest-degree term of the polynomial is nevertheless simple enough to detect nonempty varieties.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grv-prop-nonempty">
      <title>Effective classes and the polynomial subring</title>
      <statement><p>The following assertions hold.
        <ol>
          <li><p>If <m>X</m> is nonempty of dimension <m>d</m>, the highest total-degree term of <m>E_k([X])</m> is <m>c(uv)^d</m>, where <m>c</m> is the number of <m>d</m>-dimensional irreducible components. In particular, <m>[X]\ne0</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>The homomorphism <m>\Z[t]\to\Rk</m>, <m>t\mapsto\LL</m>, is injective.</p></li>
          <li><p>For nonempty <m>X,Y</m>, <m>[X][Y]\ne0</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p></statement>
      <proof><p>Stratify <m>X</m> into smooth irreducible quasiprojective pieces. Each top-dimensional piece has a smooth projective compactification whose boundary has smaller dimension. The top term of a smooth projective connected <m>d</m>-fold is <m>(uv)^d</m>; all boundary and smaller-stratum terms have lower total degree. This proves the first assertion by induction on dimension. For the second, a relation <m>f(\LL)=0</m> would give <m>f(uv)=0</m> in <m>\Z[u,v]</m>, forcing <m>f=0</m>. Finally, the product <m>X\times Y</m> is nonempty, so the first assertion applies to its class.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>For example, <m>E_k(\LL-1)=uv-1</m> differs from <m>E_k(2(\LL-1))=2uv-2</m>, proving the distinction in <xref ref="grv-ex-etale-cover" text="custom">the étale-cover calculation</xref>. More generally, equality of the classes of two nonempty varieties determines their common dimension and the number of top-dimensional irreducible components. This use of dimension applies to actual varieties through their Hodge polynomials; it is not an additive map <m>\Rk\to\Z</m> and does not recover their birational types.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-anchor-positive-regular">The polynomial subring <m>\Z[\LL]\subseteq\Rk</m> allows nonzero polynomials to be distinguished. Injectivity of multiplication by such a polynomial on all virtual classes is a stronger property: <m>\LL</m> will have a nonzero annihilator in <xref ref="grv-ch-zero-divisors"/>. Likewise, the nonvanishing of every product <m>[X][Y]</m> for nonempty varieties leaves open the possibility of an annihilating difference of classes.</p>
    <p xml:id="margin-grv-positive-regular">For integers, the inclusion <m>\Z\hookrightarrow\Rk</m> proves that no nonzero multiple of the unit vanishes. Torsion-freeness of the additive group would require <m>na=0</m> to imply <m>a=0</m> for every virtual class <m>a</m>, a question distinct from this inclusion.</p>
  </section>
</chapter>
